<!--
    The pairing dialog, and the two Settings rows that lead to it.

    Every accessibility service is a promise about what it will look at, and the promise is this
    declaration: a service that names no packages is registered for every app on the device, which
    is what a scanner calls a screen reader that can read a bank app, and it is the difference
    between this and the fork this flow came from. `com.android.settings` is where phones put
    "Pair with device", `com.android.tv.settings` is where TVs put it and
    `com.google.android.apps.wearable.settings` is where Wear OS puts it; all three are needed
    here because this fork reads the dialog on phones, opens the rows that lead to it, drives the
    whole flow on TV and does the same on a watch.

    Keep in step with hostsPairingDialog() in AdbPairingAccessibilityService, which applies the
    same rule to the windows it walks: a device whose dialog is hosted by some other settings app
    needs that name added here, or the service is never handed its events at all. Wear OS was
    exactly that case: the service was enabled, but the watch's settings app is not
    `com.android.settings`, so the reader was never told the dialog had appeared.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:packageNames="com.android.settings,com.android.tv.settings,com.google.android.apps.wearable.settings"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
/>
